For any $s,r \geqslant 1$ and $\delta \in (0,1)$, 
	given a deterministic protocol $\ensuremath{\pi}_r$ for instances $G_r \sim \ensuremath{\mathcal{G}}_r(n_r)$, with the following parameters:
	\begin{equation*}
		\ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{round}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_r) = r \qquad \ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{bw}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_r) = s \qquad \ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{suc}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_r, \ensuremath{\mathcal{G}}_r(n_r))\geqslant \delta, 
	\end{equation*}
	we can construct a deterministic protocol $\ensuremath{\pi}_{r-1}$ which takes instances  $G_{r-1} \sim \ensuremath{\mathcal{G}}_{r-1}(n_{r-1})$ and has the following parameters:
	\begin{equation*}
		\ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{round}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_{r-1}) = r-1 \qquad \ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{bw}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_{r-1}) \leqslant s \qquad \ensuremath{\textnormal{\textsf{suc}}}(\ensuremath{\pi}_{r-1}, \ensuremath{\mathcal{G}}_{r-1}(n_{r-1}))\geqslant \delta - \frac1{n_{r-1}} - 15 \sqrt{\frac{s}{n_{r-1}}}.
	\end{equation*}
